==============================================================================
2. KILL CRITERION — rank-matched controls
==============================================================================
  A club only gains by managing the clock for a man it means to hold six
  years, so the effect must be LARGER for ranked men. Asked as one model:
  does the line coefficient DIFFER between ranked and unranked men?

  top-100  x line   interaction 1.250  CI 0.649-2.410  Wald p=0.505  LRT p=0.498
  top-30   x line   interaction 0.814  CI 0.313-2.117  Wald p=0.672  LRT p=0.677
  top-10   x line   interaction 0.961  CI 0.196-4.711  Wald p=0.961  LRT p=0.961

  The interaction is the only specification here with usable power, and it
  buys that power with an assumption the subset fits never make: that absent
  a service clock a ranked man follows the SAME April promotion shape as an
  unranked one. Section 4 tests exactly that assumption.

