8. Sensitivity — the choices that could have made this come out otherwise
==============================================================================
  a. peak rank known at promotion time, against peak rank over the whole
     ranking window (which lets a rank published AFTER the debut season in):
     top-100 observed-deep    known at promotion 0.529 (n=70)   whole window 0.505 (n=71)
     top-30 observed          known at promotion 1.413 (n=34)   whole window 1.511 (n=35)

  b. the window groups. The censored years capture fewer ranked men because
     the lists that would have named them do not exist. Coding those men as
     unranked is the trap the prospect-value spike measured at a 35%
     overstatement, so they never enter a ranked-versus-unranked comparison:
     base cohort by window: {"censored":287,"observed-shallow":148,"observed-deep":399}
     censored           15 of 287 promotions are top-30 (5.2%)
     observed-shallow   10 of 148 promotions are top-30 (6.8%)
     observed-deep      24 of 399 promotions are top-30 (6.0%)
     The censored share is lower because the early lists are missing, not
     because those men were worse. A season fixed effect absorbs it.

